      "thm:maximal-converse"
    ],
    "justification": "The iff statement identifies the largest economically rational relaxation rather than offering another sufficient compliance restriction.",
    "gap": "Blackwell--Pashley impose coordinate-specific receipt, while Goff supplies a generic criterion and a two-factor boundary rather than this three-factor maximal support.",
    "consumer": "The theorem tells factorial-IV analysts exactly which interactive uptake behaviors leave every perfect-complier factorial estimand intact.",
    "proof_tex": "First, the classification theorem shows that the seven separable maps \\(g_{C_0}\\) with \\(C_0\\ne U\\) lie in \\(\\mathcal N_3\\), while \\(g_U=g^\\star\\).  Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_3\\subseteq\\mathcal E_3. \\tag{28}\n\\]\nSuppose \\(\\mathcal M\\subseteq\\mathcal E_3\\).  Under the support assumption, the signed-identification theorem gives, for every \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\mu_t^\\star=\\frac{m_t}{p_t},\n\\]\nwhere \\(m_t\\) and \\(p_t\\) are functionals of \\(P_{\\mathrm{obs}}\\).  Therefore two admissible laws with the same observed law have the same eight means.  This proves outcome-nonrestrictive identification, including unrestricted dependence of potential outcomes on response type.\n\nConversely, suppose \\(\\mathcal S_3\\subseteq\\mathcal M\\) but \\(\\mathcal M\\not\\subseteq\\mathcal E_3\\).  Choose \\(h\\in\\mathcal M\\setminus\\mathcal E_3\\).  The maximal-converse theorem supplies two admissible laws supported on\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_3\\cup\\{h\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal M\n\\]\nwith the same \\(P_{\\mathrm{obs}}\\), positive perfect-complier share, and different values of at least one \\(\\mu_t^\\star\\).  This contradicts outcome-nonrestrictive identification as defined.  Thus identification implies \\(\\mathcal M\\subseteq\\mathcal E_3\\), proving the equivalence.\n\nBy (28), \\(\\mathcal E_3\\) itself is an admissible support containing \\(\\mathcal S_3\\), and the forward argument shows it identifies the target.  The converse says every other identifying support containing \\(\\mathcal S_3\\) is a subset of \\(\\mathcal E_3\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal E_3\\) is the unique inclusion-maximal support with the stated property.  The Möbius-coefficient lemma additionally shows that retaining all separable maps uniquely forces the signed recovery coefficients, so the recovery functional is not selected by fitting the enlarged class.  Finally, the classification counts \\(|\\mathcal S_3|=8\\) and \\(|\\mathcal E_3|=29\\), establishing the claimed strict enlargement while the identified target remains the same eight-dimensional vector.",
